<div class="bg-zinc-950 text-zinc-100">
  <.marketing_nav current_user={@current_user} current={:docs} />

  <section class="py-12 sm:py-16">
    <.docs_layout
      current="scim"
      toc={[
        {"enable", "Enabling directory sync"},
        {"okta", "Okta setup"},
        {"entra", "Microsoft Entra setup"},
        {"jumpcloud", "JumpCloud setup"},
        {"keycloak", "Keycloak"},
        {"google-workspace", "Google Workspace"},
        {"group-role", "Group → role mapping"},
        {"security-notes", "Security notes"}
      ]}
    >
      <.docs_header current="scim">
        <:dek>
          Directory sync (SCIM 2.0) lets your identity provider push the user lifecycle to emisar:
          provision people from the directory, and when someone is offboarded there, their emisar
          access is revoked automatically — sessions ended, API keys revoked, no manual cleanup.
        </:dek>
      </.docs_header>

      <.docs_callout kind={:note} title="Set up SSO login first">
        <p>
          Directory sync builds on a configured SSO connection. If you haven't connected your IdP
          yet, start with <.link
            navigate={~p"/docs/sso"}
            class="text-brand-400 hover:text-brand-300"
          >Single sign-on</.link>, then enable directory sync on that connection.
        </p>
      </.docs_callout>

      <.docs_callout kind={:note} title="Where this flow stands">
        <p>
          emisar speaks standards-based SCIM 2.0, and the full provision / update / deactivate /
          reactivate lifecycle plus group&nbsp;→&nbsp;role recompute is verified end to end against
          a live standards IdP. The Okta and JumpCloud walkthroughs below were each run against a
          real tenant of that provider — Okta provisioned a user into emisar, and JumpCloud's
          activation (which creates, renames and deletes a probe group, and provisions and removes a
          test user) completes against it.
        </p>
      </.docs_callout>

      <.docs_h2 id="enable">Enabling directory sync</.docs_h2>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        On a configured connection, enable <strong class="text-zinc-200">directory sync</strong>
        to let your IdP push the user lifecycle to emisar over SCIM 2.0 — the standard Okta, Entra,
        and JumpCloud all speak as a provisioning client. The directory-sync panel lives on each
        connection's page, right under its settings. <.plan_note tier={:enterprise} />
      </p>

      <.docs_screenshot
        src="/images/docs/sso/sso-directory-sync.webp"
        title="emisar console · Directory sync (SCIM)"
        caption="A configured connection: the SCIM base URL to copy, token rotation, and the three steps for pointing your IdP at it."
        alt="The Directory sync (SCIM) panel on a configured connection: an enabled badge, Rotate token and Disable buttons, the SCIM base URL with a copy button, and the expanded 'Point your IdP at this connection' guide — enable SCIM provisioning at your provider, set that base URL as the connector's endpoint and paste the bearer token as its API token, and map the SCIM externalId to the same value the OIDC sub claim carries."
      />

      <ol class="mt-8 space-y-6">
        <li class="flex gap-4">
          <span class="grid h-8 w-8 flex-none place-items-center rounded-full bg-brand-500/20 text-sm font-semibold text-brand-300">
            1
          </span>
          <div class="min-w-0 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
            <p class="font-semibold text-zinc-100">
              Click <span class="text-zinc-300">Enable directory sync</span> on the connection.
            </p>
            <p class="mt-1.5">
              emisar shows you two things to wire into your provider's SCIM connector — both visible
              in the panel above:
            </p>
            <ul class="mt-2 space-y-2">
              <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
                <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
                <span>
                  the <strong class="text-zinc-200">SCIM base URL</strong>
                  — <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">/scim/v2</code>
                  on your emisar host (copy button beside it), and
                </span>
              </li>
              <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
                <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
                <span>
                  a <strong class="text-zinc-200">bearer token</strong>, shown
                  <strong class="text-zinc-200">once</strong>
                  when you enable or rotate it. Like every emisar secret it's write-only after that
                  — copy it then. Didn't catch it? Use
                  <strong class="text-zinc-200">Rotate token</strong>
                  to mint a fresh one (the old one stops working).
                </span>
              </li>
            </ul>
          </div>
        </li>
        <li class="flex gap-4">
          <span class="grid h-8 w-8 flex-none place-items-center rounded-full bg-brand-500/20 text-sm font-semibold text-brand-300">
            2
          </span>
          <div class="min-w-0 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
            <p class="font-semibold text-zinc-100">
              Point your IdP's SCIM connector at that base URL and bearer.
            </p>
            <p class="mt-1.5">
              In your provider, set the SCIM endpoint to the base URL and paste the token as the API
              token — emisar accepts it in the
              <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">Authorization</code>
              header. Enable Create / Update / Deactivate. The connection's
              <strong class="text-zinc-200">"Point your IdP at this connection"</strong>
              guide names the per-provider specifics. Expect provisioning to live in its
              <em>own</em>
              app: Okta's OIDC login app can't do SCIM at all, and an Entra app registration can't
              provision either. JumpCloud is the exception — one custom application can carry both.
            </p>
          </div>
        </li>
        <li class="flex gap-4">
          <span class="grid h-8 w-8 flex-none place-items-center rounded-full bg-brand-500/20 text-sm font-semibold text-brand-300">
            3
          </span>
          <div class="min-w-0 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
            <p class="font-semibold text-zinc-100">
              Match the SCIM externalId to the OIDC subject.
            </p>
            <p class="mt-1.5">
              Configure your IdP so the SCIM
              <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">externalId</code>
              carries the same stable identifier as the connection's identifier claim. Then a
              member's SSO login and their synced directory record resolve to one emisar identity —
              no email-matching anywhere. If the two sides can't be made to share an identifier,
              that connection can't be reconciled this way.
            </p>
            <ul class="mt-2 space-y-2">
              <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
                <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
                <span>
                  <strong class="text-zinc-200">Okta</strong>
                  usually lines up untouched: it populates
                  <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">externalId</code>
                  with the Okta user id, and ID tokens from its <em>org</em>
                  authorization server carry that same value as <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">sub</code>. Okta documents
                  that in examples rather than as a guarantee, and a custom authorization server
                  lets an admin rewrite the subject claim.
                </span>
              </li>
              <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
                <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
                <span>
                  <strong class="text-zinc-200">Entra</strong>
                  needs both sides changed: remap
                  <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">externalId</code>
                  from its default
                  <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">mailNickname</code>
                  to <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">objectId</code>, and set
                  this connection's identifier claim to
                  <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">oid</code>
                  — Entra's <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">sub</code>
                  is per-application, so the login app and the provisioning app would disagree.
                </span>
              </li>
            </ul>
            <p class="mt-2">
              Whatever the provider, check both values against one real user before you roll out.
            </p>
          </div>
        </li>
      </ol>

      <p class="mt-6 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Once it's wired, here's what the sync does:
      </p>
      <ul class="mt-3 space-y-3 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
          <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
          <span>
            <strong class="text-zinc-200">Provisioning</strong>
            creates the user and their membership at the connection's default role — so account
            access is in place before their first login. Runner access follows the connection's
            Default runner access setting, which starts at <em>no runners</em>: a provisioned member
            sees the fleet only once you grant it, per member or by group mapping.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
          <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
          <span>
            <strong class="text-zinc-200">Deprovisioning</strong>
            — when the IdP marks a user inactive or removes them — <strong class="text-zinc-200">suspends
            the member</strong>: their sessions are killed and their API keys revoked immediately,
            so an offboard in your directory revokes emisar access automatically. It does
            <strong class="text-zinc-200">not</strong>
            delete the user — the audit trail is preserved. Reactivation in the directory restores
            access.
          </span>
        </li>
      </ul>

      <.docs_screenshot
        src="/images/docs/sso/scim-synced-users.webp"
        title="emisar console · Synced users"
        caption="Once your IdP is connected, provisioned members appear here with the directory id they came from and the role their groups earned them."
        alt="The Synced users list on a connection: four members provisioned over SCIM, each with a SCIM badge, the directory externalId they were provisioned under, when they last synced, a locked role chip showing the role their group mapping earned them, and a Suspend action."
      />

      <.docs_callout kind={:warn} title="Using Google Workspace?">
        <p>
          Google Workspace is a first-class OIDC <em>login</em>
          provider here, but it has no outbound SCIM — it won't push the user lifecycle to
          emisar. On its own you get just-in-time provisioning on first login and <strong class="text-zinc-200">no automatic deprovisioning</strong>. To close that gap,
          route Google through your IdP (Okta, Entra, or JumpCloud) and connect <em>that</em>
          to emisar's SCIM — an offboard in Google then reaches emisar as the standard
          <code class="rounded bg-zinc-950 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">active: false</code>
          signal and access is revoked automatically, exactly as above. Most orgs already
          federate Google through an IdP, so there's usually nothing new to stand up. (Native
          Google directory sync without an IdP is on our roadmap — talk to us if you need it.)
        </p>
      </.docs_callout>

      <.docs_h2 id="okta">Okta setup</.docs_h2>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Directory sync attaches to a provider you have already connected, so set up
        <.link
          navigate={~p"/docs/sso#okta"}
          class="text-brand-300 underline decoration-brand-500/40 underline-offset-4 hover:text-brand-200"
        >Okta single sign-on</.link>
        first.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        The exact path, verified against a live Okta org. You need an Okta administrator and an
        emisar owner or admin, with the connection's
        <strong class="text-zinc-200">SCIM base URL</strong>
        and <strong class="text-zinc-200">bearer token</strong>
        from the steps above to hand.
      </p>

      <.docs_callout kind={:note} title="SCIM lives in its own Okta app">
        <p>
          Okta doesn't allow SCIM provisioning on an OIDC integration — so your sign-in app and your
          provisioning app are two separate integrations. That's Okta's documented position, not an
          emisar limitation. The provisioning app's own sign-on never has to work; it exists only to
          carry the SCIM config.
        </p>
      </.docs_callout>

      <ol class="mt-8 space-y-10">
        <li class="flex gap-4">
          <span class="grid h-8 w-8 flex-none place-items-center rounded-full bg-brand-500/20 text-sm font-semibold text-brand-300">
            1
          </span>
          <div class="min-w-0 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
            <p class="font-semibold text-zinc-100">Add the SCIM 2.0 Header Auth app.</p>
            <p class="mt-1.5">
              In the Okta Admin Console go to
              <strong class="text-zinc-200">Applications → Applications → Browse App Catalog</strong>
              and search for <strong class="text-zinc-200">SCIM 2.0</strong>. Pick
              <strong class="text-zinc-200">SCIM 2.0 Test App (Header Auth)</strong>
              — Header Auth is the variant that sends the token the way emisar reads it — then <strong class="text-zinc-200">Add Integration</strong>. Name it something an
              administrator will recognise later, take the defaults on the Sign-On tab, and finish.
            </p>
            <.docs_screenshot
              src="/images/docs/sso/okta-scim-catalog.webp"
              title="Okta Admin Console · Browse App Integration Catalog"
              caption="Four SCIM test apps share this name — take the 2.0 Header Auth one."
              alt="Okta's Browse App Integration Catalog with SCIM 2.0 Test App typed into the search box, showing a typeahead list of SCIM 2.0 Test App (Basic Auth), SCIM 2.0 Test App (Header Auth), SCIM 2.0 Test App (OAuth Bearer Token), SCIM 1.1 Test App (Header Auth), and SCIM 1.1 Test App (Basic Auth)."
            />
          </div>
        </li>
        <li class="flex gap-4">
          <span class="grid h-8 w-8 flex-none place-items-center rounded-full bg-brand-500/20 text-sm font-semibold text-brand-300">
            2
          </span>
          <div class="min-w-0 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
            <p class="font-semibold text-zinc-100">
              Point it at emisar and test the credentials.
            </p>
            <p class="mt-1.5">
              On the app's <strong class="text-zinc-200">Provisioning</strong>
              tab choose <strong class="text-zinc-200">Configure API Integration</strong>, tick <strong class="text-zinc-200">Enable API integration</strong>, and fill in the two
              values emisar gave you: the SCIM base URL as <strong class="text-zinc-200">Base URL</strong>, and the bearer as <strong class="text-zinc-200">API Token</strong>. Paste the token exactly as emisar
              showed it — Okta sends it as a bare
              <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">Authorization</code>
              header with no <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">Bearer</code>
              keyword, and emisar accepts that because the
              <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">ems-</code>
              prefix makes it unambiguous. Then click
              <strong class="text-zinc-200">Test API Credentials</strong>
              before saving.
            </p>
            <.docs_screenshot
              src="/images/docs/sso/okta-scim-verified.webp"
              title="Okta Admin Console · Test API Credentials"
              caption="Okta calls emisar directly with the bearer, so a pass here means the credential and URL are both right."
              alt="Okta's SCIM API integration form after a successful test: a green banner reading SCIM 2.0 Test App (Header Auth) was verified successfully, with Enable API integration ticked, the emisar SCIM base URL in the Base URL field, a masked API Token, and Import Groups enabled."
            />
            <p class="mt-4">
              If it fails, it's the credential or the URL: a token that was truncated on the way
              over, or a base URL missing its
              <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">/scim/v2</code>
              suffix. emisar answers a bad bearer with a plain <em>"The SCIM bearer token is missing,
              malformed, or not authorized"</em>, which Okta shows you verbatim — rotate the token in
              emisar and paste the fresh one.
            </p>
          </div>
        </li>
        <li class="flex gap-4">
          <span class="grid h-8 w-8 flex-none place-items-center rounded-full bg-brand-500/20 text-sm font-semibold text-brand-300">
            3
          </span>
          <div class="min-w-0 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
            <p class="font-semibold text-zinc-100">Turn on the lifecycle operations.</p>
            <p class="mt-1.5">
              Still under <strong class="text-zinc-200">Provisioning</strong>, open
              <strong class="text-zinc-200">To App</strong>
              → <strong class="text-zinc-200">Edit</strong>
              and enable <strong class="text-zinc-200">Create Users</strong>, <strong class="text-zinc-200">Update User Attributes</strong>, and <strong class="text-zinc-200">Deactivate Users</strong>. Deactivate is the one that
              makes offboarding automatic, and it covers reactivation too. Leave
              <strong class="text-zinc-200">Sync Password</strong>
              off — emisar has no passwords to sync.
            </p>
            <.docs_screenshot
              src="/images/docs/sso/okta-scim-to-app.webp"
              title="Okta Admin Console · Provisioning to App"
              caption="Without these three, the integration authenticates but never pushes anything."
              alt="Okta's Provisioning to App settings in edit mode, listing Create Users, Update User Attributes, Deactivate Users and Sync Password, each with an Enable checkbox."
            />
          </div>
        </li>
        <li class="flex gap-4">
          <span class="grid h-8 w-8 flex-none place-items-center rounded-full bg-brand-500/20 text-sm font-semibold text-brand-300">
            4
          </span>
          <div class="min-w-0 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
            <p class="font-semibold text-zinc-100">Assign people, then push groups.</p>
            <p class="mt-1.5">
              Assignment is what actually provisions someone:
              <strong class="text-zinc-200">Assignments → Assign → Assign to People</strong>
              (or to a group). Each assigned person appears in emisar under
              <strong class="text-zinc-200">Synced users</strong>
              at the connection's default role. To drive roles from directory groups, use the
              app's own <strong class="text-zinc-200">Push Groups</strong>
              tab — that's a separate tab, not part of the To App settings — and then map those
              groups to roles in emisar, below.
            </p>
          </div>
        </li>
      </ol>

      <.docs_callout kind={:note} title="Okta lines up on identity by default.">
        <p>
          Okta sends the Okta user id as the SCIM <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">externalId</code>, and ID tokens from
          its <em>org</em>
          authorization server carry the same value as
          <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">sub</code>
          — so sign-in and directory sync converge on one emisar identity without extra mapping. Two
          things break that: a <strong class="text-zinc-200">custom</strong>
          authorization server, whose subject claim an admin can rewrite, and access tokens, whose
          <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">sub</code>
          is often an email. Use the org authorization server, and check one real user before you
          roll out.
        </p>
      </.docs_callout>

      <.docs_h2 id="entra">Microsoft Entra setup</.docs_h2>

      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Directory sync attaches to a provider you have already connected, so set up
        <.link
          navigate={~p"/docs/sso#entra"}
          class="text-brand-300 underline decoration-brand-500/40 underline-offset-4 hover:text-brand-200"
        >Microsoft Entra single sign-on</.link>
        first. You need the <strong class="text-zinc-200">SCIM base URL</strong>
        and <strong class="text-zinc-200">bearer token</strong>
        from the steps above.
      </p>

      <.docs_callout kind={:note} title="Written from Microsoft's documented flow">
        <p>
          These steps come from Microsoft's own provisioning documentation plus emisar's Entra
          handling, which our test suite exercises — they have not yet been walked on a live Entra
          tenant the way the Okta and JumpCloud sections were. Screenshots land when we certify one.
        </p>
      </.docs_callout>

      <ol class="mt-8 space-y-10">
        <li class="flex gap-4">
          <span class="grid h-8 w-8 flex-none place-items-center rounded-full bg-brand-500/20 text-sm font-semibold text-brand-300">
            1
          </span>
          <div class="min-w-0 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
            <p class="font-semibold text-zinc-100">Create the enterprise application.</p>
            <p class="mt-1.5">
              Provisioning does not live on the app registration you made for sign-in — it needs a
              separate object. Go to <strong class="text-zinc-200">Enterprise applications → New application → Create your own application</strong>, name it <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">emisar</code>, and choose <strong class="text-zinc-200">Integrate any other application you don't find in the gallery</strong>.
            </p>
          </div>
        </li>
        <li class="flex gap-4">
          <span class="grid h-8 w-8 flex-none place-items-center rounded-full bg-brand-500/20 text-sm font-semibold text-brand-300">
            2
          </span>
          <div class="min-w-0 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
            <p class="font-semibold text-zinc-100">Point it at emisar.</p>
            <p class="mt-1.5">
              On the new application's <strong class="text-zinc-200">Provisioning</strong>
              blade set <strong class="text-zinc-200">Provisioning Mode</strong>
              to <strong class="text-zinc-200">Automatic</strong>, then fill in the two values emisar gave you —
              the SCIM base URL as <strong class="text-zinc-200">Tenant URL</strong>
              and the bearer as <strong class="text-zinc-200">Secret Token</strong>:
            </p>
            <.docs_code phx-no-format label="Tenant URL" copy_text="https://emisar.dev/scim/v2">https://emisar.dev/scim/v2</.docs_code>

            <.docs_screenshot
              src="/images/docs/sso/entra-provisioning.webp"
              title="Microsoft Entra · Enterprise application provisioning"
              caption="The Provisioning blade of the enterprise application — a different object from the app registration that handles sign-in. Connect your application is where the Tenant URL and Secret Token go."
              alt="The emisar SCIM enterprise application's Provisioning overview in the Entra portal, offering New configuration, Start provisioning and Pause provisioning in its command bar, with a Get started panel whose Create configuration step has a Connect your application button."
            />
            <p class="mt-4">
              Press <strong class="text-zinc-200">Test Connection</strong>. Entra probes the endpoint for a user that
              cannot exist, using a random GUID, and expects an empty result rather than an error —
              emisar answers that correctly, so a failure here means the URL or the token is wrong,
              not the schema. Save.
            </p>

            <.docs_screenshot
              src="/images/docs/sso/entra-provisioning-credentials.webp"
              title="Microsoft Entra · New provisioning configuration"
              caption="Leave the authentication method on Bearer. Secret token takes the ems- bearer, and Test connection sits directly beneath — Entra will not let you Create until it passes."
              alt="Entra's New provisioning configuration form for the emisar SCIM enterprise application: Select authentication method set to Bearer authentication, Tenant URL filled with https://emisar.dev/scim/v2, a masked Secret token field, and a Test connection button above a disabled Create button."
            />
          </div>
        </li>
        <li class="flex gap-4">
          <span class="grid h-8 w-8 flex-none place-items-center rounded-full bg-brand-500/20 text-sm font-semibold text-brand-300">
            3
          </span>
          <div class="min-w-0 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
            <p class="font-semibold text-zinc-100">Remap externalId to objectId.</p>
            <p class="mt-1.5">
              Open
              <strong class="text-zinc-200">Mappings → Provision Microsoft Entra ID Users</strong>
              and change the attribute feeding
              <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">externalId</code>
              from <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">mailNickname</code>
              to <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">objectId</code>.
            </p>
            <p class="mt-4">
              Do not skip this. Entra's default is <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">mailNickname</code>, which is not guaranteed to exist, is not guaranteed to be
              unique, and can change — and it is not the value your sign-in connection identifies
              people by. With <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">oid</code>
              on the SSO side and
              <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">mailNickname</code>
              here, the same person arrives as two different identities: one provisioned by the
              directory, one created when they sign in.
              <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">objectId</code>
              is the same immutable id the
              <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">oid</code>
              claim carries, so the two halves converge.
            </p>
          </div>
        </li>
        <li class="flex gap-4">
          <span class="grid h-8 w-8 flex-none place-items-center rounded-full bg-brand-500/20 text-sm font-semibold text-brand-300">
            4
          </span>
          <div class="min-w-0 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
            <p class="font-semibold text-zinc-100">Assign the people to sync.</p>
            <p class="mt-1.5">
              Under <strong class="text-zinc-200">Users and groups</strong>, assign whoever should exist in emisar, then
              turn <strong class="text-zinc-200">Provisioning Status</strong>
              on. Entra syncs on its own schedule — roughly every 40 minutes — so the first members
              appear under <strong class="text-zinc-200">Synced users</strong>
              on the emisar connection after that cycle, not instantly. Their Entra groups become
              mappable to roles below.
            </p>
          </div>
        </li>
      </ol>

      <.docs_h2 id="jumpcloud">JumpCloud setup</.docs_h2>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Directory sync attaches to a provider you have already connected, so set up
        <.link
          navigate={~p"/docs/sso#jumpcloud"}
          class="text-brand-300 underline decoration-brand-500/40 underline-offset-4 hover:text-brand-200"
        >JumpCloud single sign-on</.link>
        first.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Verified against a live JumpCloud tenant. You need a JumpCloud administrator and the
        <strong class="text-zinc-200">SCIM base URL</strong>
        and <strong class="text-zinc-200">bearer token</strong>
        from the steps above.
      </p>

      <.docs_callout kind={:note} title="One app can do both">
        <p>
          Unlike Okta and Entra, JumpCloud lets a single custom application carry sign-in and
          provisioning together: tick <strong class="text-zinc-200">Manage Single Sign-On (SSO)</strong>, pick <strong class="text-zinc-200">Configure SSO with OIDC</strong>, and tick
          <strong class="text-zinc-200">Export users to this app</strong>
          on the same step. Watch the SSO sub-choice — it defaults to <strong class="text-zinc-200">SAML</strong>, so an app you meant to be OIDC will be SAML
          unless you change it.
        </p>
      </.docs_callout>

      <ol class="mt-8 space-y-10">
        <li class="flex gap-4">
          <span class="grid h-8 w-8 flex-none place-items-center rounded-full bg-brand-500/20 text-sm font-semibold text-brand-300">
            1
          </span>
          <div class="min-w-0 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
            <p class="font-semibold text-zinc-100">Create a custom application.</p>
            <p class="mt-1.5">
              <strong class="text-zinc-200">Access → SSO Applications → Add New Application</strong>, then
              the <strong class="text-zinc-200">Custom Application</strong>
              tile. On the options step tick both
              <strong class="text-zinc-200">Manage Single Sign-On (SSO)</strong>
              — which reveals a <strong class="text-zinc-200">SAML / OIDC</strong>
              choice, defaulting to SAML — and <strong class="text-zinc-200">Export users to this app (Provisioning)</strong>, which is
              the directory push. Leave <strong class="text-zinc-200">Import users</strong>
              off: it pulls people <em>from</em>
              emisar into JumpCloud, which is the opposite of what you want. Name it and save.
            </p>
            <.docs_screenshot
              src="/images/docs/sso/jumpcloud-scim-options.webp"
              title="JumpCloud · Select Options"
              caption="Export users is the direction that matters — JumpCloud becomes the authority for the identity."
              alt="JumpCloud's Create New Application Integration wizard on the Select Options step, offering four checkboxes: Manage Single Sign-On (SSO), Import users from this app (Provisioning), Export users to this app (Provisioning), and Add a bookmark (no SSO)."
            />
          </div>
        </li>
        <li class="flex gap-4">
          <span class="grid h-8 w-8 flex-none place-items-center rounded-full bg-brand-500/20 text-sm font-semibold text-brand-300">
            2
          </span>
          <div class="min-w-0 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
            <p class="font-semibold text-zinc-100">Point its Provisioning tab at emisar.</p>
            <p class="mt-1.5">
              Open the saved app's <strong class="text-zinc-200">Provisioning</strong>
              tab (JumpCloud's own docs also call it <em>Identity Management</em>). Choose
              <strong class="text-zinc-200">SCIM API</strong>
              and <strong class="text-zinc-200">Bearer token</strong>, then fill
              <strong class="text-zinc-200">Base URL</strong>
              with emisar's SCIM base URL and <strong class="text-zinc-200">Token Key</strong>
              with the bearer. <strong class="text-zinc-200">Test User Email</strong>
              must be an address that doesn't exist in emisar yet — JumpCloud provisions and then
              deletes it as a live check, so an existing member would fail activation.
            </p>
          </div>
        </li>
        <li class="flex gap-4">
          <span class="grid h-8 w-8 flex-none place-items-center rounded-full bg-brand-500/20 text-sm font-semibold text-brand-300">
            3
          </span>
          <div class="min-w-0 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
            <p class="font-semibold text-zinc-100">Test Connection, then Activate.</p>
            <p class="mt-1.5">
              Press <strong class="text-zinc-200">Test Connection</strong>, then <strong class="text-zinc-200">Activate</strong>. Activation is not a formality: it
              creates, renames and deletes a probe group, and provisions and removes the test user,
              against your live emisar. Do <strong class="text-zinc-200">not</strong>
              press Save instead — JumpCloud discards the configuration and you start over.
            </p>
            <p class="mt-4">
              Then bind the people you want synced: the app's
              <strong class="text-zinc-200">User Groups</strong>
              tab decides who is in scope. Members appear under
              <strong class="text-zinc-200">Synced users</strong>
              on the emisar connection, and their JumpCloud groups become mappable to roles below.
            </p>
          </div>
        </li>
      </ol>

      <.docs_h2 id="keycloak">Keycloak</.docs_h2>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Keycloak cannot drive directory sync. Its built-in SCIM support runs the other way — the
        <strong class="text-zinc-200">SCIM Realm API</strong>
        added in 26.6 lets something else provision users <em>into</em>
        Keycloak — and an outbound client that pushes users to an application like emisar is on the
        roadmap, not in a release. So Keycloak gives you
        <.link
          navigate={~p"/docs/sso#keycloak"}
          class="text-brand-300 underline decoration-brand-500/40 underline-offset-4 hover:text-brand-200"
        >single sign-on</.link>
        with members created on first sign-in, and nothing else: no group → role mapping, and nobody
        removed from emisar when they leave Keycloak. Disabling the Keycloak user stops them signing
        in, which is the control that matters; remove the member in
        <strong class="text-zinc-200">Team</strong>
        to close out the rest.
      </p>

      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        The connection still offers directory sync, because emisar's endpoint is ordinary SCIM 2.0
        with a bearer token and a third-party Keycloak provisioning plugin can drive it. If you run
        one, enable sync here and point it at the base URL and token above. That plugin is yours to
        choose, configure, and support — we don't ship or certify one.
      </p>

      <.docs_h2 id="google-workspace">Google Workspace</.docs_h2>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Google Workspace cannot drive directory sync into emisar. Google provisions to SaaS apps in
        its own catalog, and a custom OAuth client — which is what
        <.link
          navigate={~p"/docs/sso#google-workspace"}
          class="text-brand-300 underline decoration-brand-500/40 underline-offset-4 hover:text-brand-200"
        >Google Workspace single sign-on</.link>
        creates — is not one of them, so there is no SCIM connector to point here. Members are
        created on first sign-in at the connection's default role, and nothing removes them
        automatically: suspending the Google account stops them signing in, which is the control
        that matters, then remove the member in <strong class="text-zinc-200">Team</strong>
        to close out their API keys. emisar's connection page hides the sync panel for this
        provider rather than offer a feature that could never connect.
      </p>

      <.docs_h2 id="group-role">Group → role mapping</.docs_h2>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        With directory sync on, map an IdP group (by its SCIM <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">externalId</code>) to an emisar role
        — <strong class="text-zinc-200">viewer</strong>, <strong class="text-zinc-200">operator</strong>, <strong class="text-zinc-200">billing manager</strong>,
        or <strong class="text-zinc-200">admin</strong>. A member in several mapped groups gets the
        <strong class="text-zinc-200">highest</strong>
        role mapped to any of them.
        <strong class="text-zinc-200">Owner is never assignable through
        sync</strong>
        — it stays a deliberate human assignment.
      </p>

      <.docs_screenshot
        src="/images/docs/sso/scim-group-role-mapping.webp"
        title="emisar console · Group → role mapping"
        caption="Each mapping names a directory group by its externalId and the role its members land at. A synced group you don't map leaves its members at the connection's default role."
        alt="The Group to role mapping panel on a connection: two mappings — the Platform Engineers directory group (externalId kc-grp-platform) mapped to Admin, and SRE On-call (kc-grp-sre) mapped to Operator — each with Edit and Delete, plus an Add mapping button."
      />

      <.docs_h2 id="security-notes">Security notes</.docs_h2>
      <ul class="mt-4 space-y-3 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
          <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
          <span>
            The
            <strong class="text-zinc-200">SCIM bearer token is an admin-grade credential</strong>
            — it can provision and deprovision across the account. Treat it like a password, and
            rotate it from the connection's panel if it's ever exposed (your IdP loses access until
            you paste the new one).
          </span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
          <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
          <span>
            Every directory-sync action lands in the
            <.link
              navigate={~p"/docs/audit-and-siem"}
              class="text-brand-300 hover:text-brand-200"
            >
              audit log
            </.link>
            with its authentication method, so you can see that the directory, not a person,
            suspended an offboarded member.
          </span>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </.docs_layout>
  </section>

  <.marketing_footer />
</div>
